{"acceptance_length": 3.4833333333333334, "acceptance_lengths": [1, 7, 1, 3, 2, 2, 1, 3, 2, 5, 5, 1, 3, 7, 3, 4, 1, 5, 8, 7, 1, 7, 6, 4, 1, 7, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 4, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 8, 8, 1, 8, 4, 8, 8, 3, 8, 8, 7, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2], "accepted_draft_lengths": [0, 6, 0, 2, 1, 1, 0, 2, 1, 4, 4, 0, 2, 6, 2, 3, 0, 4, 7, 6, 0, 6, 5, 3, 0, 6, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 3, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 7, 7, 0, 7, 3, 7, 7, 2, 7, 7, 6, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 19823088640, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 19823085056, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "You can write a Python function to check if there is any **common element** between two sequences using Python's built-in `set` data structure. Here's a simple and efficient implementation:\n\n```python\ndef overlapping(seq1, seq2):\n    return bool(set(seq1) & set(seq2))\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- `set(seq1)` and `set(seq2)` convert the sequences into sets.\n- The `&` operator finds the intersection of the two sets.\n- `bool(...)` converts the result to a boolean: `True` if there is at least one common element, `False` otherwise.\n\n### Test Cases:\n```python\nassert overlapping([1,2,3,4,5], [6,7,8,9]) == False\nassert overlapping([1,2,3], [4,5,6]) == False\nassert overlapping([1,4,5], [1,4,5]) == True\n```\n\nAll of these assertions will pass with this implementation.", "decode_seconds": 3.3633119706064463, "draft_calls": 60, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 129, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "bd20c784e546e8778a6062a600364411a7c946387693eb0712123eae85d2a6e5", "method": "relay_two_control", "output_hash": "8b447edcb3fbfb5b43b46e24e15d0a1c51e6113e51116e57ee8116437272e634", "output_tokens": 210, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 19951417344, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 20149436416, "problem_id": "Mbpp/414", "profile_regions_ms": {"draft": 1396.1657320261002, "prefill_full_target": 33.940704345703125, "prefill_relay": 0.20934399962425232, "relay": 9.96371203660965, "unattributed_runtime": 51